%[text] %[text:anchor:T_7FB15AF4] # One-dimensional linear basis functions
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] Construction of the linear one-dimensional basis functions (alternative known as hat basis functions) using the one-dimensional [Lagrange polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange_polynomial)
%[text] 
%[text] Go to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter1_BasisFunctions.m) Section in the main driver script *One-dimensional linear basis functions*
%[text] Go to [NEXT](file:.\main_Chapter12_OneDimensionalQuadraticBasisFunctions.m) Section *One-dimensional quadratic basis functions*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Dr.-Ing. Andreas Apostolatos**
%[text] **Date: 10.06.2026**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F203EBCC] ## Preamble
vars = ["X0" "XL" "numElementsL1" "mshL1"];
str = "This m-file should be ran from parent m-file ..." + ...
    "main_Chapter1_BasisFunctions.m";
for ii = 1:length(vars)
    if ~exist(vars(ii), 'var') 
        error(strcat("Variable ", vars(ii), " is not defined. ", str));
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A94A6F20] ## Definition of the linear Lagrange Polynomials
%[text] The [Lagrange Polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange_polynomial) are defined in one-dimension as follows:
%[text] $l\_j(x) = \\prod\_{1 \\le m \\le k, m \\neq j} \\frac{x - x\_m}{x\_j - x\_m}$
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![linear_mesh.png](text:image:5a38)
syms l1L1(X, X1, X2) l2L1(X, X1, X2)
l1L1(X, X1, X2) = (X - X2)/(X1 - X2);
l2L1(X, X1, X2) = (X - X1)/(X2 - X1);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A041F9B5] ## Visualization of the one-dimensional linear Lagrange Polynomials in the physical space of  the mesh
for ii = 1: length(mshL1.elements(:, 1))
    nodesEl = mshL1.nodes(mshL1.elements(ii, :));
    fplot([l1L1(X, nodesEl(1, 1), nodesEl(2, 1)) l2L1(X, nodesEl(1, 1), nodesEl(2, 1))], ... %[output:9b5e6bf3]
        [nodesEl(1, 1), nodesEl(2, 1)]); %[output:9b5e6bf3]
    if ii == 1
        hold on;
    end
end
xlabel("X") %[output:9b5e6bf3]
ylabel("l_i") %[output:9b5e6bf3]
hold off; %[output:9b5e6bf3]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0A9C686E] ## Computation of the Lagrange polynomials in the parametric space
%[text] The parametric space of each element in this case is defined as $\\xi \\in \[-1, 1\]$:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![map_to_biunit_domain_linear.png](text:image:8bb7)
syms xi N1L1(xi) N2L1(xi) N(xi)
N1L1(xi) = l1L1(xi, -1, +1);
N2L1(xi) = l2L1(xi, -1, +1);
NL1(xi) = [N1L1(xi) N2L1(xi)];
fplot([N1L1 N2L1], [-1 +1]) %[output:902b064a]
legend(["N_1^{L1}", "N_2^{L1}"]); %[output:902b064a]
xlabel("\xi") %[output:902b064a]
ylabel("N_i^{L2}") %[output:902b064a]
%[text] ![](text:image:98f8) **Reflect**
%[text] All elements in the finite element mesh share the same parametric space

